Why Do We Need NSGs?

Imagine you have three VMs:

VM1 (Web)
VM2 (Application)
VM3 (Database)

Without any firewall:

Internet
     │
     ▼
 All Traffic Allowed ❌

This is insecure.

With an NSG:

Internet
     │
     ▼
Allow HTTPS (443)
Allow SSH (22) from Admin IP
Deny Everything Else

The NSG acts like a firewall for Azure resources.

What is a Network Security Group (NSG)?

An NSG is a Layer 3/Layer 4 firewall that filters network traffic to Azure resources.

It controls:

Source
Destination
Port
Protocol
Direction
Action (Allow/Deny)
NSG Architecture
Internet
     │
     ▼
Public IP
     │
     ▼
NSG
     │
     ▼
VM

Only traffic allowed by the NSG reaches the VM.

NSG Rule Components

Every rule has:

Property	Example
Priority	100
Source	Internet
Source Port	*
Destination	VM
Destination Port	443
Protocol	TCP
Action	Allow
Rule Priority

Azure processes rules from the lowest priority number to the highest.

Priority 100 → Checked first
Priority 200 → Checked second
Priority 300 → Checked third

Once a rule matches, Azure stops evaluating further rules.

Example
Priority	Rule
100	Allow HTTPS (443)
200	Allow SSH (22)
300	Deny All

Traffic to port 443 matches rule 100 and is allowed. Rule 300 is never evaluated.

