Default NSG Rules

Azure automatically creates default rules.

Inbound
Priority	Rule	Action
65000	AllowVNetInBound	Allow
65001	AllowAzureLoadBalancerInBound	Allow
65500	DenyAllInBound	Deny
Outbound
Priority	Rule	Action
65000	AllowVNetOutBound	Allow
65001	AllowInternetOutBound	Allow
65500	DenyAllOutBound	Deny

You cannot delete these default rules, but you can override them with custom rules using a lower priority number.

Custom NSG Example

Suppose you have a web server.

Rules:

Priority	Port	Action
100	443	Allow
110	80	Allow
120	22	Allow from Admin IP only
4096	*	Deny

This means:

Users can access the website.
Only administrators can SSH.
All other inbound traffic is blocked.
NSG Association

An NSG can be associated with:

Subnet
Network Interface (NIC)
Subnet-level NSG
VNet
 │
 ▼
Web Subnet
     │
     ├── VM1
     ├── VM2
     └── VM3

One NSG protects all VMs in the subnet.

NIC-level NSG
VM1
 │
 ▼
NIC
 │
 ▼
NSG

Only that VM is affected.

Which Should You Use?
Scenario	Best Choice
Same rules for many VMs	Subnet NSG
Different rules per VM	NIC NSG

In enterprise environments, Subnet NSGs are much more common because they are easier to manage.

What is an Application Security Group (ASG)?

An ASG lets you group VMs logically instead of writing rules based on IP addresses.

Without ASG
Allow:
10.0.1.4
10.0.1.5
10.0.1.6

If IPs change, rules must be updated.

With ASG
Web Servers
      │
      ▼
ASG-Web

NSG Rule:

Source = Internet
Destination = ASG-Web
Port = 443
Allow

Any VM added to ASG-Web automatically follows the rule.

